Purchasing a vehicle is a significant investment. When serious defects appear and repeated repairs fail to solve the problem, understanding available legal protections becomes essential. A [Lemon Law attorney](/) can help evaluate whether a vehicle qualifies for relief under California Lemon Law and explain the options available to consumers.

California’s Lemon Law provides important safeguards for owners and lessees of defective vehicles. From determining eligibility requirements to pursuing buybacks, replacements, or refunds, the law offers remedies when manufacturers cannot repair substantial defects within a reasonable number of attempts. At CLINE APC, we focus exclusively on California Lemon Law matters and help consumers navigate the legal process with confidence.

How California Lemon Law Protects Consumers

California Lemon Law, formally known as the Song-Beverly Consumer Warranty Act, protects consumers who purchase or lease defective vehicles covered by a manufacturer’s warranty. The law requires manufacturers to repair qualifying defects within a reasonable number of attempts.

When repairs fail to resolve a defect that substantially affects a vehicle’s use, value, or safety, the manufacturer may be required to repurchase or replace the vehicle. The law applies to many vehicle types, including:

- Recreational vehicles

- Certain boats

Coverage may extend to both new and used vehicles when manufacturer warranty protection remains active.

Eligibility Criteria for a California Lemon Law Claim

Not every repair issue qualifies under California Lemon Law. Several factors help determine whether a vehicle meets the legal requirements.

The Defect Must Be Significant

The defect must impair the vehicle’s use, value, or safety. Minor cosmetic concerns generally do not qualify. Examples of qualifying issues may include:

- Engine failures

- Transmission defects

- Brake malfunctions

- Steering problems

- Electrical system failures

- Airbag defects

- Persistent warning lights connected to major systems

Multiple Repair Attempts

Manufacturers must receive a reasonable opportunity to repair the problem. A vehicle may qualify when:

- The same defect remains unresolved after multiple repair visits

- Safety-related defects persist after repair attempts

- Different repair efforts fail to correct an ongoing issue

Repair records are valuable evidence when evaluating whether a manufacturer had sufficient opportunities to fix the defect.

Extended Time Out of Service

California law also considers the total number of days a vehicle remains unavailable due to warranty-covered repairs.

A vehicle may qualify if it has been out of service for repairs for a cumulative total of 30 days or more, even when those days result from different repair visits.

The Defect Cannot Result From Misuse

The manufacturer is generally responsible only for defects arising from manufacturing or warranty-related issues. Damage caused by unauthorized modifications, accidents, neglect, or misuse may not qualify under Lemon Law protections.

The Step-by-Step Process of Filing a Lemon Law Claim in California

Many consumers are unfamiliar with the legal process involved in pursuing a Lemon Law claim. Understanding the general steps can help clarify what to expect.

Gather Vehicle Documentation

Documentation forms the foundation of any claim. Important records include:

- Purchase or lease agreements

- Warranty documents

- Repair orders

- Service invoices

- Communications with dealerships

- Communications with manufacturers

These documents help establish the history of the defect and the manufacturer’s repair efforts.

Review Eligibility Requirements

A detailed review of repair history helps determine whether the vehicle meets California Lemon Law standards.

At CLINE APC, we examine repair records, warranty coverage, service history, and defect details to evaluate potential claims.

File the Claim

Once eligibility has been established, legal action may begin against the manufacturer. The claim outlines the defect history, repair attempts, and requested remedies under California law.

Negotiate Resolution

Manufacturers may choose to resolve claims through settlement discussions. Potential resolutions can include vehicle repurchases, replacement vehicles, reimbursement of certain expenses, or financial compensation.

Litigation When Necessary

Some cases require court proceedings when manufacturers dispute liability. Experienced trial attorneys can present evidence, challenge manufacturer defenses, and pursue available remedies under the law.

Common Defective Vehicle Issues Covered by California Lemon Law

Vehicle defects vary widely, but certain categories appear frequently in Lemon Law claims.

Powertrain Problems

Issues involving the engine, transmission, drivetrain, or fuel system can significantly affect vehicle reliability and performance. Common examples include:

- Engine stalling

- Excessive oil consumption

- Transmission slipping

- Failure to accelerate properly

- Persistent check-engine warnings

Electrical System Defects

Modern vehicles depend heavily on complex electrical systems. Electrical failures can affect safety features, vehicle operation, and overall functionality. Examples include:

- Battery drainage

- Faulty sensors

- Malfunctioning infotainment systems

- Software defects

- Electrical shorts

Brake and Steering Defects

Problems affecting steering or braking systems raise serious safety concerns. Potential issues include:

- Loss of steering control

- Brake failure

- Brake warning lights

- Power steering malfunctions

- Excessive vibration during braking

Safety System Failures

Safety-related defects may qualify after fewer repair attempts because of the risks they create. Examples include:

- Airbag malfunctions

- Seatbelt failures

- Backup camera defects

- Collision avoidance system failures

- Sudden acceleration issues

